## 1. Introduction
Perfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) have been detected in water as an emerging class of highly recalcitrant contaminants due to their application in fire retardants, packing materials, nonstick cookware and paper coatings [1,2]. However, they are difficult to biologically degrade in natural environments because of the high bond energy of the carbonfluorine bond (C-F, $544\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ ) and the high redox potential of fluorine $\mathrm{~\bar{E}~}^{0}=3.6\mathrm{~V})$ [3]. Studies have shown that PFAS, including perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA), can be detected in many environmental matrices and even in human tissues. Diseases such as thyroid disease [4], testicular cancer [5] and kidney cancer [6] are closely related to PFOA levels. Therefore, efficient and cost-effective degradation methods for PFOA pollutants are urgently needed.
Advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) have been widely used to degrade organic pollutants through free radicals. Compared with AOPs, other methods, including electrochemical (EC) oxidation [7], ultrasonic treatment [8] and photocatalysis [9], have disadvantages such as complex operation, high energy consumption or inefficiency. To reduce the energy consumption and improve the degradation efficiency, AOPs are generally combined with other degradation methods. For example, photocatalysis-based AOPs, utilize UV irradiation with peroxymonosulfate (PMS) or persulfate (PS) to degrade PFOA in solutions. PMS and PS can be activated to produce sulfate radicals $(\mathrm{SO}_{4}{\bullet}^{-})$ by transition metal catalysts and UV irradiation. Recently, Wu [10] et al. applied PS-assisted photocatalytic ozonation to improve the degradation efficiency of PFOA. Xu [11] et al. used a $\mathrm{PMS}/\mathrm{Ga_{2}O_{3}}/\mathrm{UV}$ system to degrade PFOA and showed excellent removal rates within $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ and $60\ \mathrm{min}$ under $254~\mathrm{nm}$ and $185~\mathrm{{nm}}$ UV irradiation, respectively. UV-based AOPs showed efficient degradation, but an enhanced degradation rate under visible light is desirable because of the lower energy consumption. Improving the degradation rate of PFOA under visible light irradiation based AOPs is a favorable approach.
Owing to its unique porous structure and large surface area, metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention in the treatment of wastewater. The recent reports demonstrated that the MOFs have immense potential in AOPs processes by activating PMS to produce free radicals. Zhang et al. [12] achieved effective degradation of methylene blue via the zeolitic imidazolate frameworks-9 (ZIF-9) in the presence of PMS. Furthermore, through modifying the metal ions and organic linkers, several kinds of MOFs can also achieve outstanding photocatalytic ability by utilizing UV or visible light [13,14]. Therefore, utilization of the MOFs which possessed both PMS activating ability and photocatalytic activities to construct the novel photocatalytic assisted– AOPs system may enhance wastewater treatment capacity. Besides, compared with single-metallic MOFs, the catalytic performance of bimetallic MOFs (bi-MOFs) can be significantly improved because of the generated defects and the cooperative effect between the different metals [15,16]. Sun et al. [17] indicated that the Fe/Mn-bi-MOFs showed higher catalytic performance than the original Fe-MOFs in degradation of phenol. Based on these advantages, choosing the bi-MOFs which owned both PMS activating ability and photocatalytic activities to degrade PFOA in the photocatalytic assisted–AOPs system can exhibit expectant performance.
However, due to the high surface energy of bi-MOFs nanoparticles, it is inevitable to aggregate, which leads to the insufficient performance of biMOFs catalysis. Therefore, it is very necessary to choose applicable matrix for relieving agglomeration. Immobilization of bi-MOFs via an in-situ solvothermal method can achieve a good distribution of the MOFs in the matrix to reduce aggregation. Furthermore, it is the good way to control the catalytic reaction by loading bi-MOFs on fiber materials because of separating easily. Zhou [18] et al. reported the particle growth of zeolitic imidazolate frameworks-8 (ZIF-8) on electrospun polyurethane (PU) nanofibers via an in-situ process. As byproduct of the paper industry, lignin is the second most abundant, non-toxic and biocompatible natural polymer. Its highly functionalized macromolecules are able to obtain of biodegradable biomass base materials in the form of gels or membranes [19]. In addition, the lignin-based nanofiber membranes (lignin/PVA) that are fabricated via the typical electrospinning method are strong enough to grow MOFs by the solvothermal method. Meanwhile, it is also less expensive to use lignin instead of cellulose nanofibers in some cases. Nevertheless, it is difficult to conversion into fibers nanofibers from aqueous solutions of lignin by electrospinning techniques owing to the low molecular mass and viscosity of pure lignin solution [20,21]. For this problem, Dallmeyer [22] et al. reported it is feasible to prepare lignin-based electrospun nanofibers by dissolving lignin in dimethylformamide (DMF). However, the plasticizer poly(acrylonitrile) (PAN) that is used is unsustainable. Therefore, less expensive and environmentally friendly PVA and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) are good substitutes [23].
Inspired by the above idea, we fabricated lignin-based nanofiber composite membranes that contain Co/Fe MOFs via a typical electrospinning and in-situ solvothermal method. Furthermore, we evaluated the membrane performance for PFOA degradation with PMS under solar light irradiation. The photocatalysis and AOPs showed two simultaneous catalytic activities during the degradation process due to the properties of Co/Fe MOFs. This study mainly aimed to characterize Co/Fe MOFs nanoparticles and composite membranes in detail, to explore the catalytic performance of composite membranes for PFOA under different conditions and to examine the catalytic mechanism and the intermediate products during the degradation process.
## 2. Experimental methods
## 2.1. Reagents and materials
Cobalt nitrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ , 1,4-dicarboxybenzene (terephthalic acid: TPA), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), HPLC-grade acetonitrile and hydrochloric acid (HCl) were purchased from Damao Chemical Reagent Factory. Iron (III) acetylacetonate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3},$ ) and perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) were obtained from Aladdin Co., China. Potassium peroxymonosulfate (PMS, $2\mathrm{KHSO}_{5}\cdot\mathrm{KHSO}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ was purchased from Macklin Inc., China. Polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, average Mw $130,000{\mathrm{g.mol}}^{-1}$ ) was obtained from Sigma-Aldrich, China. Anhydrous ethanol (EtOH) and tertbutanol (TBA) were obtained from Ante Food Co., Ltd. The black liquor was obtained from paper based functional materials laboratory of Shaanxi University of Science and Technology. All chemical reagents were directly used without further purification and all solutions were prepared using ultra-pure water, obtained from a Milli-Q system.
## 2.2. Preparation of lignin and purification
The lignin was prepared by direct acidic precipitation from black liquor (Fig. 1a). HCl (6 M) was added to the black liquor to adjust the pH value $\left(\mathrm{pH2.0}\right)$ under vigorous stirring at room temperature. After centrifugation of the solution, the obtained lignin was washed three times with dilute HCl $\mathrm{(pH}2.0)$ and vacuum dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ over-night [24].
## 2.3. Preparation of spinning solutions and lignin-based electrospun nanofibers
The lignin:PVA mass ratio was from 80:20 to 50:50 and total weight percent ratio is $25\mathrm{wt\%}$ in DMSO. Typically, a fixed PVA amount was slowly added and dissolved in DMSO under vigorous stirring at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ A certain amount of lignin was simultaneously added to the mixed solution under stirring at $65^{\circ}C$ for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ when PVA was completely dissolved [23]. After cooling naturally, the solution mixture was electrospun (Fig. 1b). The liquid precursors were electrospun in electrospun equipment (Fig. S1) at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $43\%\mathrm{RH}$ experimental environment. In a typical electrospinning process, the spinneret inner diameter was $0.40\mathrm{mm}$ and aluminum foil was used as collectors. The distance and DC voltage between the tip of the spinneret and the collectors were $15\mathrm{cm}$ and $15~\mathrm{kV}$ , respectively. After that, the lignin/PVA membranes were carefully peeled off from the aluminum foil (detailed information can be obtained in the Supporting information).
## 2.4. Preparation of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs composite membrane
Co/Fe MOFs were obtained according to a procedure by Jin P·K et al. with a slight modification [25] and the preparation of lignin/PVA/biMOFs was illustrated in Fig. 1c. Typically, $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ ( $\dot{\mathrm{~1~mM}}$ and Co $(\mathsf{N o}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{:}0.5\ \mathrm{mM}^{\cdot}$ ) were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solution that the volume ratio of DMF: EtOH was 5:3. Then, the lignin/PVA membranes $2\mathsf{c m}\times5\mathsf{c m})$ were immersed in the mixed solution for 30 min. Next, TPA $\cdot1.67\mathrm{mM})$ was tardily dissolved in the obtained solution under mechanical agitation for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, the homogeneous mixture and lignin/PVA membrane were transferred into a $100\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon stainless autoclave and maintained at $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ After being cooled to room temperature, the composite membranes were washed with EtOH and deionized water three times and freeze dried.
## 2.5. Characterization methods
## 2.6. Degradation experiment and analysis
A Teflon reactor vessel was filled with $50\mathrm{mL}$ of PFOA aqueous solution $(20~\mathrm{mg/L})$ at room temperature. Three pieces of a certain size of composite membranes were immersed in PFOA solution for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then, a certain amount of PMS was added into PFOA solution and the performance for PFOA degradation evaluated under UV, solar light and darkness, respectively. During degradation process, the UV lamps (9 W, the wavelength of photoexcitation is $185~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) were placed $5\ \mathrm{cm}$ above from the PFOA solution or the reaction system was irradiated under solar light at 11:00 am to $2{:}00~\mathrm{pm}$ . Meanwhile, the reaction solution was well mixed by magnetic stirring [11]. At $30\mathrm{min}$ intervals, $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the solution was extracted from reaction system and immediately filtered. The concentration of PFOA in different samples was measured by HPLC equipped with a UV detector. During the degradation process, the intermediates were analyzed by UHPLC-MS (detailed information can be obtained in the Supporting information). Finally, the recyclability of composite membranes were evaluated after rinsing and freeze-dried.
The kinetics of the degradation was obtained by fitting the firstorder kinetic mode $\mathrm{~R}^{2}>0.90\AA$ and was described by the following Eq. (1):
$$
\ln\left(\frac{C_{0}}{C_{t}}\right)=k t
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Basic properties of the lignin/PVA composite membrane
To explore the properties of lignin/PVA composite membranes of different lignin:PVA mass ratios, composite membranes of different mass ratios were prepared. Due to the viscosity of the precursor solution with increasing ratios of PVA, electrospinning became difficult when the PVA ratio was less than $30\%$ or when it exceeded $50\%$ . These lignin/PVA composite membranes were fabricated with lignin:PVA mass ratios from 80:20 to 50:50 at a $25\mathrm{wt\%}$ total concentration in DMSO. These composite membranes were studied using scanning electron microscopy (SEM), dynamic contact angle and absorption test and tests with a servo material testing machine.
The SEM images of obtained lignin/PVA composite membranes are showed in Fig. S2. Obvious lignin beads were observed when the mass ratio of lignin:PVA was 80:20. Additionally, the precursor fluid was not able to form nanofiber membranes by electrospinning at this ratio. The lignin beads were gradually converted into uniform nanofibers when the ratio of PVA was increased. As shown in Fig. S2b-d, the average diameter of the nanofibers was $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , $240~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which demonstrated that the nanofiber diameter increased with the PVA ratio. The appropriate wettability of the lignin/PVA composite membranes is beneficial for making contact with PFOA during degradation [7]. Hence, the water contact angles of the obtained lignin/PVA composite membranes were measured and presented in Fig. S3. The water contact angles for the ratios of 70:30, 60:40, and 50:50 were $54.7^{\circ}\pm0.9^{\circ}$ , $62.6^{\circ}\pm1.9^{\circ}$ and $60.9^{\circ}\pm1.4^{\circ}$ , respectively, suggesting that the degree of hydrophilicity changed little with the lignin:PVA mass ratios. This phenomenon was attributed to the presence of hydrophilic hydroxyl functional groups in both lignin and PVA. Moreover, the ideal strength of the lignin/PVA composite membrane is a necessary condition for the in-situ growth of Co/Fe MOFs. Therefore, the tensile strength of the obtained lignin/PVA composite membranes was measured and the results are presented in Fig. S4. The tensile strength of composite membranes at mass ratios of lignin:PVA from 70:30 to 50:50 was $(6.8\pm0.8)\$ MPa, $(23.6\pm1.2)\$ MPa and $(31.2\pm1.6)\$ MPa, respectively. The results indicated that the strength of lignin/PVA composite membranes increased with increasing ratios of PVA within the tested ratios. This phenomenon can be explained by the increasing density of hydrogen bonds in the lignin/PVA composite membranes and the nanofiber diameter [23].
To obtain a suitable wettability and tensile strength as well as a higher composite membrane yield, the studied system consisted of an initial precursor solution of 60:40 (lignin:PVA) and the total weight percent ratio of lignin and PVA in DMSO is $25\mathrm{wt\%}$ in all the following sections of this work.
## 3.2. Morphologic and structure characterization
The morphological structures of the lignin/PVA composite membranes and lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs composite membranes were shown in Fig. 2 (image magnification $10,000{\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{i}}$ . The SEM image in Fig. 2a shows that the lignin/PVA composite membranes were successfully prepared by electrospinning with a uniform fiber diameter. The average diameter of lignin/PVA nanofibers was approximately $240\pm12\mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the SEM image in Fig. 2b shows that the Co/Fe MOFs were loaded on the lignin/PVA composite membranes by the in-situ solvothermal method, and agglomeration of Co/Fe MOFs was not observed. Compared with pure Co/Fe MOFs nanoparticles (the insets in Fig. 2b), the morphological structure of Co/Fe MOFs loaded on the surface of lignin/PVA composite membranes had no significant changes. These nanofibers were also coated with growth limited Co/Fe MOFs due to insufficient space. In summary, this phenomenon indicated that the lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs composite membranes were successfully prepared by the in-situ solvothermal method.
FTIR spectra of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs were obtained to analyses the kinds of functional groups (Fig. 3a). The characteristic peaks at $3429\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was associated with the hydroxyl group (OH) stretching vibration of lignin and PVA. The peak at $1597\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was assigned to the aromatic skeletal vibration of lignin and the organic ligands of Co/Fe MOFs. The characteristic peaks at 1263, 1128 and $1022\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the C\ O of the guaiacyl ring, C\ C and C\ O stretching vibrations, and the aromatic ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ in plane deformation, respectively. The peaks at 690 and $540\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were characteristic of the Co\ O stretching vibration and Fe\ O stretching vibrations in Co/Fe MOFs, respectively [14,23,26–28].
XRD patterns of pure Co/Fe MOFs, lignin/PVA and lignin/PVA/ bi-MOFs (Fig. 3b) were collected and a variety of sharp peaks were observed. The obvious diffraction peaks of pure Co/Fe MOFs were positioned at $2\theta=)$ $10.2^{\circ}$ , $11.5^{\circ}$ $13.9^{\circ}$ , $16.4^{\circ}$ , $17.3^{\circ}$ , $17.7^{\circ}$ , $18.3^{\circ}$ , $20.5^{\circ}$ , $24.7^{\circ}$ and $46.7^{\circ}$ , which indicated that the pure Co/Fe MOFs samples were well crystallized [29,30]. The lignin was comprised of a phenylpropane unit, and these units further formed a three dimensional polymer network. As shown in Fig. 3b, a broad peak was located at (2θ $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\mathbf{\dot{\Gamma}}$ $19.5^{\circ}$ , which demonstrated that the lignin was partially crystalline [31]. The XRD pattern of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs showed peaks similar to those of pure Co/Fe MOFs and lignin/PVA. The FTIR spectra and XRD results also shows that the pure Co/Fe MOFs were successfully loaded onto lignin/PVA composite membranes.
Furthermore, the lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs were characterized by XPS spectroscopy to explore the surface composition and the changes of chemical valence. In the XPS survey spectra of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs (Fig. S5), these peaks of C, O, Co and Fe can be simultaneously observed. Nevertheless, due to the crystal lattice of Co/Fe MOFs, only a few Fe and
Co elements were detected on the surface of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs [32]. In the XPS spectra of O 1s (Fig. 4a), two peaks at 531.8 and $533.9\mathrm{eV}$ were found, which correspond to Fe-O/Co-O and C\ O, respectively. In the XPS spectra of C 1s (Fig. 4b), two major peaks were attributed to C\ C (284.3 eV) and C\ O (286.6 eV) [33–35].
## 3.3. Optical properties
To investigate the optical adsorption properties, the ultraviolet– visible diffuse reflectance spectra (UV–vis DRS) of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs were recorded. As shown in Fig. 5a, there was a strong adsorption band response of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs in the visible light range from 400 to $650~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The optical band gap energy of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs was calculated from the Tauc plot and was approximately $2.66\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 5a, inset) [14,36]. The results indicated that the lignin/PVA/biMOFs can utilize solar light. The Mott–Schottky curve of lignin/PVA/biMOFs was measured in the electrochemical test (Fig. 5b), and the curve was used to reveal the changes in the electronic structure in lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs. Meanwhile, the materials were n–type semiconductors based on the positive slope. The flat band potential $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathbb{f}})$ of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs was around $-0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ versus SCE (equivalent to $-0.54\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE) that was calculated by the Mott–Schottky curve. In general, the conduction band (CB) position of the n–type semiconductor was more negative by $0.1\mathrm{V}$ than the flat band potential [37,38]. As a result, the CB position of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs was approximately $-0.64\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE. According to the band gap of $2.66\mathrm{~eV}$ , the valence band (VB) was calculated by the equation $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{V B}}=\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}+\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{g}}.$ The VB position of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs was approximately $2.02\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE.
As shown in Fig. 6, the charge carrier migration of lignin/PVA/ bi-MOFs was detected by photoelectrochemical experiments under irradiation of 400 W xenon lamp. A sudden change in the electrode photocurrent response could be observed when a xenon lamp was switched on or off and the value of electrode photocurrent tended to stabilize. Moreover, the variation in the electrode photocurrent response was reproducible during the detection process.
## 3.4. Catalytic degradation of PFOA
Fig. 8 shows the concentration of $\mathtt{F}^{-}$ in the reaction solution during the degradation of PFOA under solar light irradiation and the results showed that the concentration of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ increased with reaction time. This phenomenon confirmed that the C\ F bonds were able to effectively cleave under the optimal PFOA degradation conditions. The defluorination ratio in the PMS/membranes/solar light system was $49\%$ and the concentration of $\mathtt{F}^{-}$ was $6.7\mathrm{mg/L}$ after 180 min. Nevertheless, the calculated concentration of $\mathrm{\DeltaF^{-}}$ should be $12.3\mathrm{mg/L}$ when $89.6\%$ of PFOA was removed. This phenomenon was attributed to two main factors. On the one hand, a part of $\mathtt{F}^{-}$ was adsorbed on the surface of the lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs composite membranes [7]. On the other hand, a small amount of shorter-chain perfluorocarboxylates (PFCAs) was obtained as the degradation products [9]. Some shorter-chain PFCAs were detected by ultra-high-performance liquid chromatograph tandem mass spectrometer (UHPLC-MS) in this paper to confirm this claim.
## 3.5. Proposed activation mechanism and degradation pathway
## 3.5.1. Generated radical and possible mechanism
In the PMS/membranes/solar light system, photocatalysis and AOPs were two catalytic pathways during the degradation process because of the properties of Co/Fe MOFs (Fig. 9). Under solar light irradiation, the photogenerated electrons $({\mathsf e}^{-})$ can be excited from lignin/PVA/ bi-MOFs and activate $S_{2}0_{8}^{2-}$ to produce $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ radicals. More importantly, $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ can accelerate the redox cycle of Fe (II-III-II) in Co/Fe MOFs and improve the oxidation capacity of the whole system, as shown in Eqs. (2)–(6) [11,40,41], which was more beneficial to the degradation efficiency than AOPs alone. Moreover, a small amount of $0_{2}{\bullet}^{-}$ was produced from the adsorbed $0_{2}$ on the catalyst because the $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}$ of lignin/ PVA/bi-MOFs was more negative than the reduction potential of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~O~}}_{2}/$ $0_{2}{\bullet}^{-}$ $(-0.33~\mathrm{V})$ , as shown in Eq. (7). However, the lignin/PVA/biMOFs were not able to produce OH• by photocatalysis because $\mathrm{E_{VB}}$ was more negative than the reduction potential of $0\mathrm{H}^{-}/0\mathrm{H}^{\bullet}$ (2.40 V) [27]. The photogenerated ${{\mathrm{e}}^{-}}/{{\mathrm{h}}^{+}}$ from lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs would rapidly recombine. Therefore, these factors ensured that photocatalysis is not the dominant pathway in the degradation process.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{M O F s+h v\rightarrow M O F s(e^{-}+h^{+})}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{F e^{3+}+e^{-}\rightarrow F e^{2+}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{F e^{2+}+H S\mathrm{O}_{5}\rightarrow\l{F}e^{3+}+S\mathrm{O}_{4}\l^{-}+O H^{-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{S\mathrm{O}_{4}\l^{-}+S O_{4}\l^{-}\rightarrow S_{2}O_{8}\l^{2-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{S_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}\l^{2-}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow S O_{4}\l^{-}+S O_{4}\l^{2-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{O_{2}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow Q_{2}\l^{-}}}\end{array}
$$
It has been reported that transition metals can activate PMS to produce $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ and OH• [42]. To evaluate the valence state of Co and Fe elements, the XPS spectra of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ and Fe 2p on the lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs membrane surface before and after catalysis were obtained, as shown in Fig. 10. In Fig. 10a, the two main peaks located at 780.5 and $782.5\mathrm{eV}$ were attributed ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{0}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ , respectively [32]. The content of two peaks were $33.80\%$ and $14.54\%$ before catalysis, respectively. However, Fig. 10b exhibits that the content of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ were $21.83\%$ and $26.20\%$ after catalysis, respectively. This changes of content demonstrated that the catalytic reaction was initiated on the surface of the composite membranes. ${\mathrm{HSO}}_{5}^{-}$ possibly obtained electrons from $C0^{2+}$ to produce $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ radicals in the catalytic process [25]. Therefore, the relative areas of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ were observed to increase from $14.54\%$ to $26.20\%$ . Meanwhile, a small amount of electrons from ${\mathrm{HSO}}_{5}^{-}$ also were accepted by ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and was reduced to $C0^{2+}$ during the catalytic process. So the electron/charge balance on the surface of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs can be maintained by the redox cycle of Co (II-III-II) and the process was proposed as followed (Eqs. (8)–(11)) [18,43].
