To confirm which active species played a major role in the catalytic reaction, free radicals were distinguished by radical quenching experiments (Fig. 11a). EtOH quickly scavenged $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ and OH• $k_{\mathrm{SO4}}\cdot^{-}=$ $1.6{-}7.7\times10^{7}\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ; $k_{\mathrm{OH}}\bullet=1.2–2.8\times10^{9}\mathrm{M}^{-1}s^{-1})$ because of the $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto\mathbf{\alpha}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{\beta}$ in its structure. Compared with EtOH, t-BuOH effectively scavenged only OH• $\begin{array}{c c c c c c c}{{(k_{\mathrm{OH}}}}&{{\bullet=}}&{{3.8\mathrm{-}7.6}}&{{\times}}&{{10^{8}}}&{{\mathsf{M}^{-1}}}&{{\mathsf{s}^{-1}}}\end{array}$ , $\begin{array}{r l}{k_{504}\bullet^{-}}&{{}=}\end{array}$
$4.0{-}9.1\times10^{5}~\mathrm{M}^{-1}~\mathrm{}s^{-1})$ [46]. Owing to the presence of t-BuOH $(20~\mathrm{mM})$ , the degradation rate reduced by approximately $10\%$ which proved that OH• was involved but not pivotal in the catalytic reaction. Both the catalytic capability and the degradation rate reduced around $37\%$ when EtOH $\mathrm{20~mM}$ existed in reaction solution. These results revealed the presence of $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{OH}}^{\bullet}$ radicals in the reaction system, in which $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ played a more significant role in the catalytic reaction. Under dark conditions, the degradation rate also decreased by approximately $30\%$ . These phenomena proved that $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ , $\mathfrak{h}^{+}$ and $0_{2}{\bullet}^{-}$ were beneficial to the degradation of PFOA. Furthermore, EPR experiment was designed to explore the change of free radicals with 5, 5-dimethyl-1- pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) as the spin trapping agent under solar light irradiation and in the dark, respectively. As shown in Fig. 11b, the signals of $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{OH}}^{\bullet}$ were observed under different conditions. The signal intensity of free radicals was significantly enhanced under solar light irradiation because $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ can effectively decompose $S_{2}0_{8}^{2-}$ into $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ , which increased the concentration of free radicals in the reaction solution. However, obvious signals of free radicals were also detected under dark conditions due to transition metal ions activating PMS. Therefore, PFOA was also degraded by activating PMS with transition metal ions of lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs in the dark.
## 3.5.2. Decomposition pathways of PFOA
To research the degradation pathway and mechanism of PFOA in the system, samples were analyzed by UHPLC-MS analysis (Table S2, Fig. S7). Based on the experimental results and works in the literature [11,47,48], the possible pathway was shown in Fig. 12. Under solar light irradiation, $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ and other radicals were rapidly produced by transition metals and photogenerated electrons to activate PMS and $S_{2}0_{8}^{2-}$ . Then, PFOA can be oxidized into perfluorinated alkyl radicals $(\bullet{\sf C}_{7}\mathrm{F}_{15})$ by these active species (Eq. (14)). Next, $\mathsf{C}_{7}\mathsf{F}_{15}\mathsf{O H}$ was formed when the $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathsf{C}_{7}\mathsf{F}_{15}$ radicals reacted with $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in the mixed solution. Furthermore, $\mathsf{C}_{7}\mathsf{F}_{15}\mathsf{O H}$ eliminated $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ to generate ${\mathsf{C}}_{6}{\mathsf{F}}_{13}{\mathsf{C O F}}$ (Eq. (15)). Finally, ${\mathsf{C}}_{6}{\mathsf{F}}_{13}{\mathsf{C O F}}$ was further converted to $\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{F}_{13}\mathsf{C O O H}$ (PFHpA) by hydrolysis (Eq. (16)). The residual organic pollutants were degraded by a similar decomposition procedure to form shorter chain compounds.
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{S O_{4}^{\cdot-}+C_{7}F_{15}C O O H{\rightarrow}^{\cdot}C_{7}F_{15}+S O_{4}^{\ 2-}+C O_{2}+H^{+}}\\ &{}\\ &{\cdot C_{7}F_{15}+H_{2}O{\rightarrow}C_{6}F_{13}C O F+H F+H}\\ &{}\\ &{C_{6}F_{13}C O F+H_{2}O{\rightarrow}C_{6}F_{13}C O O H+H F}\end{array}
$$
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, lignin/PVA composite membranes were synthesized via a typical electrospinning method and lignin/PVA showed enough strength to grow Co/Fe MOFs nanoparticles on composite membranes through an in-situ solvothermal method. Compared with pure Co/Fe MOFs nanoparticles, lignin/PVA/bi-MOFs facilitated the dispersion of Co/Fe MOFs nanoparticles, and were beneficial for separating the catalyst from solution. Under solar light irradiation, the obtained composite membranes exhibited high performance for PFOA degradation in the presence of PMS due to photocatalysis and AOPs coexisting in two catalytic pathways. Specifically, $89.6\%$ of PFOA was degraded when the pH value was 7.0 and the dosage of PMS was 8 mmol/ L within $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ under solar light irradiation. In addition, the lignin/PVA/biMOFs composite membranes retained $77\%$ catalytic capacity after 4 cycles. The radical quenching experiments indicated that $S0_{4}{\bullet}^{-}$ radicals played an important role in the catalytic reaction and that $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ , $\mathfrak{h}^{+}$ and $0_{2}\bullet^{-}$ were beneficial to the degradation of PFOA. Meanwhile, the degradation intermediates and the detailed degradation pathway of PFOA were proposed by UHPLC-MS analysis. Therefore, the results of this study expanded the further development of biomass composite for the degradation of organic pollution. Furthermore, in the industrial perspective, the use of lignin and PVA as substrate also decrease greenhouse gas emissions, the risk of secondary pollution and cost than using others nano substrate materials during the production process.
